00:17:15.042 --> 00:17:18.912
what methods we use to mitigate this issue, prevent

111
00:17:18.962 --> 00:17:22.853
cold welding from happening. So first, two slides,

112
00:17:22.922 --> 00:17:23.162
please.

113
00:17:28.582 --> 00:17:29.632
What is cold welding?

114
00:17:30.882 --> 00:17:34.232
Cold welding is basically this phenomenon that I characterize in a really

115
00:17:34.262 --> 00:17:37.562
simplistic sense as metals are like Play-Doh.

116
00:17:39.142 --> 00:17:42.542
Obviously, not the same as Play-Doh, but when we have two

117
00:17:42.582 --> 00:17:45.642
metals that are completely clean surfaces.

118
00:17:45.962 --> 00:17:49.062
The nature of metals that makes them ductile and malleable,

119
00:17:50.182 --> 00:17:53.922
that interatomic interaction also means that when we have

120
00:17:53.942 --> 00:17:57.582
two clean pieces of metal in contact with one another, there's

121
00:17:57.642 --> 00:18:01.222
no real meaningful differentiation between those pieces of metal.

122
00:18:02.202 --> 00:18:06.152
Which means that if those conditions occur, we can basically have

123
00:18:06.162 --> 00:18:10.142
two pieces of metal fused together with very little pressure, just having surface

124
00:18:10.222 --> 00:18:14.182
contact. Now, the amount of stickiness to

125
00:18:14.242 --> 00:18:17.782
that bond will depend on time, and it will depend on pressure between the

126
00:18:17.822 --> 00:18:19.102
two pieces of metal,

127
00:18:19.942 --> 00:18:23.682
but this is something we really want to avoid when it comes to spacecraft.

128
00:18:23.802 --> 00:18:24.402
Slide, please.

129
00:18:28.382 --> 00:18:32.232
So a visual demonstration here. On the surface of Earth, we don't often

130
00:18:32.322 --> 00:18:36.092
see cold welding. It does occur, but it's kind of a niche case, which I'll talk

131
00:18:36.142 --> 00:18:37.202
about later.

132
00:18:38.282 --> 00:18:42.202
Because we have, say you had an iron ingot or a steel beam

133
00:18:42.242 --> 00:18:46.122
or what have you, all of the oxygen available in the

134
00:18:46.162 --> 00:18:50.022
atmosphere is constantly doing a very light layer of oxidation

135
00:18:50.102 --> 00:18:51.462
on the outside of that piece of metal.

136
00:18:52.062 --> 00:18:55.922
So the two pieces of metal, we take them on Earth and we put them together, and

137
00:18:55.982 --> 00:18:59.722
they don't just fuse together because they have this very thin

138
00:18:59.902 --> 00:19:03.822
shell of oxidized metal, which doesn't have that same

139
00:19:04.402 --> 00:19:08.382
set of properties that metal has. So they remain two separable

140
00:19:08.422 --> 00:19:08.922
pieces.

141
00:19:10.062 --> 00:19:13.922
If we don't have that thin oxidized layer, what

142
00:19:13.962 --> 00:19:17.802
we see is those two pieces of metal fuse together like Play-Doh.

143
00:19:18.142 --> 00:19:21.302
So you might say, now why wouldn't we just build all of these

144
00:19:21.322 --> 00:19:25.042
components on the ground, not in vacuum, so we have that thin

145
00:19:25.082 --> 00:19:28.762
layer of oxidized metal so the pieces don't fuse

146
00:19:28.782 --> 00:19:31.302
together if they come in contact when we're in vacuum?

147
00:19:31.942 --> 00:19:34.142
The thing is, we do. Slide, please.

148
00:19:35.602 --> 00:19:37.552
What we see is that--

149
00:19:39.442 --> 00:19:39.842
Slide, please.

150
00:19:44.022 --> 00:19:47.702
What we see is that we run into issues with the surfaces of the metal

151
00:19:47.762 --> 00:19:51.122
getting damaged, and as soon as we damage the surface of the metal when we're in

152
00:19:51.162 --> 00:19:54.282
vacuum, now we have some exposed

153
00:19:54.422 --> 00:19:55.682
non-oxidized metal

154
00:19:56.722 --> 00:20:00.122
that's not going to re-oxidize because we don't have available oxygen.

155
00:20:00.362 --> 00:20:03.892
And there's different effects that have names for this,

156
00:20:04.502 --> 00:20:08.141
but this is the real danger when it comes to cold welding on orbit.

157
00:20:08.762 --> 00:20:12.242
Because for the most part, once again, we already have that very thin oxidized

158
00:20:12.252 --> 00:20:15.532
layer, but if we start having metal pieces come into contact with one another,

159
00:20:16.082 --> 00:20:19.962
they're going to do small amounts of damage to each other just by these natural

160
00:20:20.002 --> 00:20:23.342
processes of coming into contact, and they're going to

161
00:20:23.382 --> 00:20:27.262
expose that non-oxidized metal, and that's where we get cold

162
00:20:27.302 --> 00:20:27.762
welding.

163
00:20:29.022 --> 00:20:29.602
Slide, please.

164
00:20:33.542 --> 00:20:37.402
So there's four primary categories of surface

165
00:20:37.482 --> 00:20:41.442
damaging. The first one here on the left, galling, is

166
00:20:41.522 --> 00:20:45.382
actually where we see cold welding occur on the ground, and we see this in really

167
00:20:45.462 --> 00:20:48.122
tight-fitting, well-machined bolts and screws.

168
00:20:49.342 --> 00:20:53.302
So you get this tight fit and you're driving the screw in and the two surfaces of

169
00:20:53.362 --> 00:20:57.122
metal are scraping against each other, kind of removing that

170
00:20:57.182 --> 00:21:00.991
oxidized metal. And because the fit's so tight, we get

171
00:21:01.042 --> 00:21:04.962
cold welding occurring because there's not enough oxygen available to

172
00:21:05.002 --> 00:21:06.882
re-oxidize that surface quickly enough.

173
00:21:07.422 --> 00:21:10.342
So galling is the one example that we tend to see on the ground.

174
00:21:10.962 --> 00:21:14.942
Bending would be like bending a piece of wire, where because the

175
00:21:14.982 --> 00:21:18.502
properties of that oxidized metal aren't the same as the non-oxidized metal, the

176
00:21:18.522 --> 00:21:20.102
surface cracks and exposes.

177
00:21:21.062 --> 00:21:24.842
Fretting would just be like vibration while in contact, and then impact

178
00:21:25.002 --> 00:21:28.912
is pretty self-explanatory. One piece impacts another, and we get

179
00:21:28.942 --> 00:21:32.142
exposure of that metal and potential for cold welding.

180
00:21:32.642 --> 00:21:33.142
Slide, please.

181
00:21:37.742 --> 00:21:41.711
So the one really big notable incident of cold welding occurring

182
00:21:41.742 --> 00:21:45.202
on orbit and causing a failure is in 1991,

183
00:21:45.922 --> 00:21:48.922
three of the ribs for the high-gain antenna for

184
00:21:49.082 --> 00:21:52.962
Galileo failed to deploy. In retrospect, we figured out

185
00:21:52.982 --> 00:21:56.942
that this was a result of cold welding, specifically because of fretting.

186
00:21:57.522 --> 00:22:01.182
So the vibration that was happening during launch and transfer into its

187
00:22:01.262 --> 00:22:05.002
orbit ended up exposing some of that non-oxidized metal,

188
00:22:05.582 --> 00:22:09.442
and cold-welded those ribs to the frame that they were attached to in their folded

189
00:22:09.482 --> 00:22:10.241
configuration.

190
00:22:12.022 --> 00:22:15.642
Generally, we don't see that there's a ton of cold welding

191
00:22:15.702 --> 00:22:18.262
issues on orbit because it's a known phenomenon.

192
00:22:18.322 --> 00:22:21.542
In fact, this has been known about-- It was characterized first in the

193
00:22:21.562 --> 00:22:25.512
1940s. So people have known about this a long time, and there's a

194
00:22:25.542 --> 00:22:29.122
lot of care put into avoiding having issues with cold welding.

195
00:22:29.202 --> 00:22:33.102
So we don't tend to see a bunch of issues, but this was a really notable example of

196
00:22:33.142 --> 00:22:35.702
that happening and causing a failure. Slide, please.

197
00:22:37.922 --> 00:22:40.102
So how do we mitigate this? How do we avoid it?

198
00:22:40.282 --> 00:22:43.792
Well, you avoid metal-on-metal contact is the short version.

199
00:22:44.662 --> 00:22:48.592
If it's impossible to avoid metal-on-metal contact in terms of the mechanisms that

200
00:22:48.621 --> 00:22:52.342
we're using, we have to assess the

201
00:22:52.362 --> 00:22:55.582
risk and we have to use coatings or surfaces to

202
00:22:55.622 --> 00:22:58.292
separate those metal surfaces.

203
00:22:59.582 --> 00:23:03.182
Cold welding databases that are available can give us a lot of information about

204
00:23:03.962 --> 00:23:07.272
how much risk is posed by this particular

205
00:23:07.522 --> 00:23:11.202
metal-on-metal contact. Different metals will have different amounts of bonding

206
00:23:11.302 --> 00:23:15.122
depending on, again, time, depending on the amount of pressure between the two,

207
00:23:15.212 --> 00:23:18.862
so on and so forth. And those databases can really give you a good idea

208
00:23:18.872 --> 00:23:22.552
about what kind of risk is being presented by this potential

209
00:23:22.562 --> 00:23:23.942
contact. Slide, please.

210
00:23:28.122 --> 00:23:31.782
But since there are so many cases where we just can't avoid that metal-on-metal

211
00:23:31.882 --> 00:23:32.342
contact,

212
00:23:33.382 --> 00:23:37.162
we use techniques like applying grease, using washers or barriers,

213
00:23:37.702 --> 00:23:41.482
and coatings. Now, grease tends to be a really good option for

214
00:23:41.562 --> 00:23:45.462
non-separable contacts, meaning that if you have

215
00:23:45.502 --> 00:23:48.162
grease between the two pieces of metal and the two pieces of metal are meant to

216
00:23:48.242 --> 00:23:50.382
come apart, the grease is not going to work well.

217
00:23:50.422 --> 00:23:52.942
It's going to actually make it harder to pull the pieces apart.

218
00:23:52.962 --> 00:23:56.462
But it's really good if the pieces are constantly against each other, like

219
00:23:56.522 --> 00:23:57.982
rotating or something like that.

220
00:23:58.882 --> 00:24:02.322
We do tend to see issues with outgassing when it comes to grease, though.

221
00:24:02.482 --> 00:24:05.102
So that is a big concern if we're looking at using grease.

222
00:24:05.842 --> 00:24:09.602
Washers and barriers, things like ceramics and polymers are nice.

223
00:24:09.762 --> 00:24:13.502
Nobody's ever seen them cold weld, so they tend to be a good

224
00:24:13.582 --> 00:24:14.882
option for offering that barrier.

225
00:24:15.012 --> 00:24:16.272
And then when we're talking about coatings,

226
00:24:17.122 --> 00:24:18.582
the thicker the coating, the better off you are.

227
00:24:19.062 --> 00:24:22.102
Obviously, there's limits to that for other reasons.

228
00:24:23.892 --> 00:24:27.752
But one of the ideals that we're looking for with the coating is

229
00:24:27.782 --> 00:24:31.642
that that coating gets integrated into the metal surface, like we see

230
00:24:31.662 --> 00:24:32.702
with parkerization.

231
00:24:33.182 --> 00:24:36.882
If you're familiar with that process, where we actually see a chemical reaction

232
00:24:36.922 --> 00:24:40.482
where we get a lot of the coating integrated into the surface of the metal.

233
00:24:41.342 --> 00:24:45.162
So even if we expose some of it, we still get a limited

234
00:24:45.222 --> 00:24:46.182
