00:42:35.948 --> 00:42:39.628
This is further complicated with the extreme temperature

500
00:42:39.688 --> 00:42:42.568
swings. Sometimes you want to be radiating

501
00:42:43.528 --> 00:42:47.148
if you're getting too hot, or you don't want to if you're in a cold environment.

502
00:42:48.308 --> 00:42:49.568
So controlling

503
00:42:50.548 --> 00:42:54.228
these materials and where the heat goes and when it gets radiated is very important

504
00:42:54.268 --> 00:42:54.748
in vacuum.

505
00:42:55.840 --> 00:42:59.280
Then here we get into the design responses for engineering

506
00:42:59.320 --> 00:43:03.300
and the structuring mechanisms. So you want to use qualified materials that

507
00:43:03.320 --> 00:43:07.219
are low outgassing, so that those gases aren't released, contamination.

508
00:43:08.520 --> 00:43:11.200
But then you also vacuum bake these components before flight.

509
00:43:12.320 --> 00:43:13.100
You want to be aware

510
00:43:15.220 --> 00:43:19.060
to vent trapped gas, even if you have low outgassing materials.

511
00:43:19.100 --> 00:43:22.980
If you have some sort of enclosure where there's just air in there, as the

512
00:43:23.020 --> 00:43:24.620
pressure decreases

513
00:43:25.460 --> 00:43:29.240
as you ascend into space towards vacuum, that trapped gas will

514
00:43:29.280 --> 00:43:32.900
expand and break or shatter whatever it's

515
00:43:33.000 --> 00:43:36.260
contained by. And then you want to use dry lubrication.

516
00:43:36.640 --> 00:43:38.610
So special space greases

517
00:43:39.840 --> 00:43:42.320
are used to make sure they don't outgas.

518
00:43:44.520 --> 00:43:48.200
As we've mentioned, controlling the temperature with the severe temperature

519
00:43:48.300 --> 00:43:50.060
swings to hot to cold is very important.

520
00:43:50.520 --> 00:43:54.100
So we use a combination of passive and active controls, different thermal

521
00:43:54.160 --> 00:43:57.700
blankets, radiators, and heat pipes to move heat and reject it

522
00:43:57.760 --> 00:44:00.840
passively. But then you can use active

523
00:44:01.620 --> 00:44:05.380
electric heaters and thermostats and louvers when you're in the

524
00:44:05.400 --> 00:44:09.180
cold cases. And then you want to qualify this

525
00:44:09.500 --> 00:44:13.100
from your design to test in thermal vacuum chambers, so called TVAC

526
00:44:13.140 --> 00:44:15.420
chambers, where you cycle through hot and cold

527
00:44:16.700 --> 00:44:20.640
to mimic that space environment in vacuum, and you

528
00:44:20.700 --> 00:44:24.600
can see how different components are performing or where the heat is, and you

529
00:44:24.760 --> 00:44:28.740
can redesign as needed. So in summary, you really need to design

530
00:44:28.760 --> 00:44:32.200
from the vacuum up. You need to treat it as your active environment, where there's

531
00:44:32.220 --> 00:44:36.040
going to be no cooling, materials are going to outgas, your

532
00:44:36.100 --> 00:44:39.960
clean metal surfaces may cold weld, and you're going to have violent temperature

533
00:44:40.080 --> 00:44:43.100
swings. So the design needs to take all these into

534
00:44:43.120 --> 00:44:46.940
consideration using the parts we talked about

535
00:44:47.060 --> 00:44:50.900
for low outgassing materials, vent pass, so no trapped airs in there,

536
00:44:51.720 --> 00:44:55.620
dry lubricants, and a combination of active and passive thermal controls.

537
00:44:56.140 --> 00:44:59.870
And then finally, TVAC testing is very important to just bake out and

538
00:45:00.100 --> 00:45:04.040
outgas all your materials, but also validate that your as-built model matches

539
00:45:04.049 --> 00:45:07.850
your thermal design, where nothing is

540
00:45:07.940 --> 00:45:11.820
exceeding its temperature profile that it's rated

541
00:45:11.880 --> 00:45:15.220
for. So that's it for my current events

542
