564
00:46:49.240 --> 00:46:52.820
Hi, everyone. I'm Paige Malden. My current events topic is vacuum

565
00:46:52.880 --> 00:46:55.480
environment and spacecraft design considerations.

566
00:46:56.060 --> 00:46:59.350
I'm using Blue Origin's Blue Moon Mark 1 lunar lander as the current

567
00:46:59.400 --> 00:47:03.140
example. It recently completed thermal vacuum testing at NASA

568
00:47:03.160 --> 00:47:07.140
Johnson Space Center. The main question I'm focusing on is simple:

569
00:47:07.180 --> 00:47:10.219
Why does vacuum testing matter before a spacecraft flies?

570
00:47:16.060 --> 00:47:19.700
For the current event, NASA announced in May 2026 that Blue

571
00:47:19.780 --> 00:47:23.440
Origin's Blue Moon Mark 1 lunar lander completed environmental

572
00:47:23.480 --> 00:47:27.460
testing. The testing was done inside Chamber A at NASA Johnson Space

573
00:47:27.500 --> 00:47:30.919
Center. That chamber was used to recreate vacuum

574
00:47:31.020 --> 00:47:33.840
conditions and extreme hot and cold temperatures.

575
00:47:34.560 --> 00:47:38.540
Engineers used the test to check how the lander performed before it is expected to

576
00:47:38.620 --> 00:47:41.750
operate near the Moon. The important point is that

577
00:47:42.720 --> 00:47:46.130
this was real flight hardware being tested against real environmental

578
00:47:46.180 --> 00:47:49.620
concerns. So this is not just a textbook topic.

579
00:47:49.900 --> 00:47:52.880
It's something spacecraft programs still have to prove before launch.

580
00:47:53.320 --> 00:47:56.910
To understand why this matters, it helps to look at what vacuum changes for a

581
00:47:57.020 --> 00:47:57.600
spacecraft.

582
00:47:59.620 --> 00:48:03.120
Space is often described as empty, but empty does not mean harmless.

583
00:48:03.620 --> 00:48:06.420
A spacecraft in vacuum is surrounded by very little pressure.

584
00:48:06.800 --> 00:48:10.640
That means trapped gas inside panels, covers, or small volumes can

585
00:48:10.660 --> 00:48:12.800
expand unless it has a controlled way to vent.

586
00:48:13.580 --> 00:48:17.520
Vacuum also removes convection. On Earth, air can carry heat

587
00:48:17.600 --> 00:48:20.660
away from warm hardware. In space, that doesn't happen.

588
00:48:21.640 --> 00:48:24.610
Heat has to move through the spacecraft by conduction or leave the

589
00:48:24.700 --> 00:48:28.400
spacecraft by radiation. Another issue is outgassing.

590
00:48:28.680 --> 00:48:32.260
Some materials release trapped gases or volatile compounds when placed in

591
00:48:32.300 --> 00:48:36.160
vacuum. Those molecules can move through vacuum and deposit on colder

592
00:48:36.260 --> 00:48:40.000
surfaces. For a spacecraft, that can create contamination concerns for

593
00:48:40.100 --> 00:48:43.180
optics, sensors, thermal controlled surfaces, and solar arrays.

594
00:48:43.960 --> 00:48:47.400
So the vacuum environment affects structures, thermal control, and material

595
00:48:47.500 --> 00:48:48.060
selection.

596
00:48:49.960 --> 00:48:52.320
I grouped the main risks into three simple areas.

597
00:48:52.740 --> 00:48:56.720
The first is thermal control. Space is cold in some ways, but spacecraft

598
00:48:56.760 --> 00:49:00.304
can still overheat. Electronics, batteries, and instruments generate

599
00:49:00.344 --> 00:49:03.424
heat, and that heat cannot be removed by surrounding air.

600
00:49:03.484 --> 00:49:06.484
The second risk is outgassing and contamination.

601
00:49:06.544 --> 00:49:10.204
This matters because even a thin film on an optical surface, a

602
00:49:10.244 --> 00:49:12.714
radiator, or a solar array can reduce performance.

603
00:49:13.244 --> 00:49:16.884
This is especially important for missions with sensors or cameras.

604
00:49:16.904 --> 00:49:19.604
The third risk is pressure and material behavior.

605
00:49:19.664 --> 00:49:23.604
Trapped volumes need vent paths. Seals, lubricants, adhesives,

606
00:49:23.644 --> 00:49:26.884
and mechanisms may behave differently in vacuum than they do on the ground.

607
00:49:27.424 --> 00:49:31.124
So the design concern is not only whether the spacecraft survives, the

608
00:49:31.204 --> 00:49:35.144
spacecraft also has to stay clean, thermally stable,

609
00:49:35.164 --> 00:49:38.904
and functional over the mission. This is where thermal

610
00:49:38.924 --> 00:49:40.584
vacuum testing becomes important.

611
00:49:40.594 --> 00:49:44.404
A chamber like NASA's Chamber A is used to recreate some of the major conditions a

612
00:49:44.464 --> 00:49:47.764
spacecraft will experience. The chamber can provide high vacuum,

613
00:49:48.444 --> 00:49:50.824
and it can also create hot and cold boundary conditions.

614
00:49:51.164 --> 00:49:54.884
During testing, engineers can operate hardware in a more flight-like environment.

615
00:49:54.914 --> 00:49:58.624
They watch temperatures, structural behavior, electronics, software responses, and

616
00:49:58.704 --> 00:50:02.304
subsystem performance. The goal is to find problems while the spacecraft is still

617
00:50:02.364 --> 00:50:06.224
on the ground. For example, a test could show one component gets too hot,

618
00:50:06.264 --> 00:50:10.004
or that the material behaves unexpectedly, or that the thermal model needs to be

619
00:50:10.044 --> 00:50:13.804
updated. Finding that during ground testing is much better than

620
00:50:13.824 --> 00:50:17.464
discovering it after launch when the vehicle's already in space and much harder to

621
00:50:17.484 --> 00:50:17.764
fix.

622
00:50:19.484 --> 00:50:21.624
Design responses are fairly practical.

623
00:50:21.664 --> 00:50:24.214
First, engineers select low outgassing materials.

624
00:50:24.744 --> 00:50:28.204
That reduces the chance that the released materials will contaminate sensitive

625
00:50:28.304 --> 00:50:32.074
surfaces. Second, hardware may be baked out before final integration or

626
00:50:32.104 --> 00:50:32.404
flight.

627
00:50:33.354 --> 00:50:37.184
That helps drive off volatile materials early instead of letting it release later

628
00:50:37.244 --> 00:50:40.764
near the spacecraft. Third, designers provide controlled vent

629
00:50:40.864 --> 00:50:44.544
paths. That prevents trapped gas from creating pressure loads inside

630
00:50:44.584 --> 00:50:48.344
enclosed spaces. Fourth, mechanisms and lubricants have to

631
00:50:48.384 --> 00:50:52.144
be vacuum compatible. A mechanism that works normally on Earth may

632
00:50:52.184 --> 00:50:56.154
not behave the same way in vacuum. Thermal control hardware is also

633
00:50:56.184 --> 00:50:59.974
a major part of design. That can include insulation,

634
00:51:00.004 --> 00:51:02.904
heaters, radiators, thermal straps, and conductive paths.

635
00:51:03.404 --> 00:51:07.284
Finally, thermal vacuum testing checks whether or not all those choices

636
00:51:07.344 --> 00:51:08.184
work together.

637
00:51:09.004 --> 00:51:12.324
The overall goal is to keep the spacecraft clean, thermally stable,

638
00:51:12.784 --> 00:51:16.404
structurally safe, and functional in flight-like vacuum conditions.

639
00:51:18.064 --> 00:51:20.984
The main takeaway is that vacuum is not just the absence of air.

640
00:51:21.394 --> 00:51:25.064
It changes how a spacecraft transfers heat, changes how the material releases

641
00:51:25.104 --> 00:51:29.084
contaminants, and it changes how pressure inside trapped volumes behaves.

642
00:51:29.684 --> 00:51:33.424
Blue Moon Mark 1 is a current example of why these effects are still important for

643
00:51:33.444 --> 00:51:37.334
spacecraft design. Before a spacecraft flies, engineers need

644
00:51:37.364 --> 00:51:40.784
evidence that the design can operate under realistic environmental conditions.

645
00:51:41.604 --> 00:51:44.624
That's why thermal vacuum testing is such an important step before

646
00:51:44.664 --> 00:51:48.184
launch. Thank you. I'm happy to take any questions.

